#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc24eade-1b3d-5672-88fd-0bb48b371509" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d34198a-cd07-5a08-8aa8-38d1e2ffa00f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292914} {:answer/id #uuid "799aeb84-bdc6-54e2-9dcf-c48d0a462d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2))-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292915} {:answer/id #uuid "885b624b-818d-5f19-866a-7b99e939245a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292916} {:answer/id #uuid "d032cadd-d126-5a42-9992-e1de343fc13a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2))-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292917} {:answer/id #uuid "b901a701-0924-534c-b204-f56250140f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b901a701-0924-534c-b204-f56250140f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292918} :answer-field/id #uuid "a27a8296-3fbc-593b-990f-f3a8856f80ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8ff1d836-eff6-5416-8eca-f38f9d49cb8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-10045" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)$ | $1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)}^{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)}^{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d359337c-4ec2-5100-bdac-d0858619db3b" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccb95fe6-9eed-5ab0-9d09-6689b1515745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\tan (x^{3}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065621} {:answer/id #uuid "ef864b1e-dd98-5a69-93c9-cd59b7cb3c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\tan (x^{3}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065622} {:answer/id #uuid "1310a41d-c6fe-5387-becf-b49736487691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\tan (x^{3}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065623} {:answer/id #uuid "38edf83e-dbd5-577c-8e43-c7123e5248e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan (x^{3}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065624} {:answer/id #uuid "43972e45-ad81-5a1a-b84a-7502ee661914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\sec (x^{3}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef864b1e-dd98-5a69-93c9-cd59b7cb3c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\tan (x^{3}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065622} :answer-field/id #uuid "69577e96-85d8-54a9-b6a1-88ddb496a806" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1978088-009a-552d-a613-5fc9a66c590e" :question/math-academy-id "q-13474" :question/problem "$\\int 4{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int {sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d359337c-4ec2-5100-bdac-d0858619db3b" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb4d2bb-fdc6-52d2-8431-faced7e42df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292921} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce02167-e712-5bf4-8f5e-56c70f44ee58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292922} {:answer/id #uuid "1b16369d-f215-5951-975b-b93fbbea1d82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292923} {:answer/id #uuid "1e2effa8-cb26-5d13-91b2-d7d58efee624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292924} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b721c0-e203-5134-a61c-c8c523281190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3b721c0-e203-5134-a61c-c8c523281190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292925} :answer-field/id #uuid "5622a881-5efd-551d-bacc-0d4f2606b172" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7b8b9fd5-920e-5e84-a786-4e2fa1bbbe7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-3038" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(1/x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(1/x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "084eb4cc-2f84-5288-90a6-be84066922b6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10be2153-58f9-5fdb-8aee-003db3a4ef43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292928} {:answer/id #uuid "464a7fee-cdae-5411-ac36-0274f045c5da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10be2153-58f9-5fdb-8aee-003db3a4ef43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292928} :answer-field/id #uuid "4665f929-297f-5960-b15f-c3fbbdc0c955" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1647abd3-8524-50dc-9015-b53f7e45b70f" :question/math-academy-id "q-350060" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}3{sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=x-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\pi /4$ | $0$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}3{sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{0} \\\\  & =3(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "084eb4cc-2f84-5288-90a6-be84066922b6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b720361-fa00-51ec-83b6-c9a264d424a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b720361-fa00-51ec-83b6-c9a264d424a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292932} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3ee8efe-9659-5bca-8f1b-6ce5a82d861d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d2e06399-eadb-50ef-a9f6-d3bbbc68b0aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-350071" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=x-\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /3$ | $2\\pi /3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u){|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-4(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =-4(1-2) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
